@*
    TEST simulation notice.

    The single most important thing an operator needs to know before pressing "Jira Kaydı Oluştur"
    is whether a real Jira issue will appear. When both providers are configured as Simulation the
    backend says so on every record, preview, and transfer response, and this states it plainly.

    Deliberately prominent and deliberately not alarming. It is not a fault, and styling it as one
    would teach operators to dismiss the one banner that changes what a button means. The wording
    comes from the server when it supplies a notice; nothing here decides that a run is simulated.
*@

@if (Active)
{
    <div class="so-simulation" role="status">
        <span class="so-simulation-icon" aria-hidden="true">
            <MudIcon Icon="@Icons.Material.Filled.Science" />
        </span>
        <div class="so-simulation-text">
            <p class="so-simulation-title">TEST SİMÜLASYONU</p>
            <p class="so-simulation-body">
                Bu işlem gerçek Turuncu Hat veya Jira sisteminde kayıt oluşturmaz.
            </p>
            @* Server-supplied wording, shown as an addition rather than a replacement so the
               guarantee above is always present even if the notice changes. *@
            @if (!string.IsNullOrWhiteSpace(Notice))
            {
                <p class="so-simulation-note">@Notice</p>
            }
        </div>
    </div>
}

@code {
    /// <summary>Whether the backend reported simulation mode for this response.</summary>
    /// <remarks>
    /// Comes from <c>simulationMode</c>. The UI never decides this for itself: a client-side
    /// simulation would be a screen that lies about what the server is going to do.
    /// </remarks>
    [Parameter]
    public bool Active { get; set; }

    /// <summary>Server-supplied notice text, when one was returned.</summary>
    [Parameter]
    public string? Notice { get; set; }
}
